{
  "items": [
    {"n": 1, "name": "First screen", "score": 1, "reason": "The headline and decision block give a clear call (keep September), the reasons (character and cost) and a Watch line. But half of the reason is cost, and the headline and the recommendation ('the lowest production cost of the three') present cost as something the model itself does. In fact the costs come from a provider routing nobody named, and an earlier round found qwen3.8-flash cheaper. That qualifier only appears further down, in Watch and in claim cost."},
    {"n": 2, "name": "Coherent story", "score": 2, "reason": "The claims follow from one another, and each sentence matches its values table: in claim character, A's pass rate is -7.6 (-12.59 to -2.57), B's is -3.0 (-7.36 to +1.34), and both holds-character rows are clear of zero. Two loose ends: Watch puts A's headroom against the margin at 2.4 points on the corrected interval, while 'What you could change' says A's verdict turns at a 10.8 margin, which implies 4.2 points of room. And claim off-lane calls off-lane September's weakest stratum, though the full table shows 'assistant shape' lower at 58.8% (only 6 cases)."},
    {"n": 3, "name": "Honest emphasis", "score": 1, "reason": "September's weak spot is shown (65.8% on off-lane asks), and B's pass-rate drop is correctly called not told apart from zero. But the confidence clauses for the judge caveat and the late-aspect caveat fall back on the production-cost gap as 'measured and does not rest on the judge'. They don't say that the gap belongs to an unnamed routing that an earlier round contradicted. So the fallback reads firmer than it is."},
    {"n": 4, "name": "Sound elision", "score": 2, "reason": "None of the omitted items would change the call. The outcome and transcript scores go the same way as the character finding. The booth, delivery and failed-reply measures are not separated for either candidate. Measuring spend is also higher for both candidates ($6.28 and $5.76 against $5.40), so it agrees with the cost story."},
    {"n": 5, "name": "Confounds handled", "score": 1, "reason": "The reasoning-share confound on 1017 (55% against 76%) is carried in claim character, claim cost, Watch and the next steps, and unknown routing has its own next step. But claim off-lane reads 1017's holds-character drop (-4.7, -8.2 to -1.3), which the reasoning confound attaches to, and neither the claim nor the confidence carries it."},
    {"n": 6, "name": "Actionable next steps", "score": 2, "reason": "Each step names an experiment and what it would settle: rework off-lane handling and rerun, calibrate the judge, confirm routing and reprice, and rerun 1017 at matched reasoning. The 815-cases figure matches the settle arithmetic (80% power). It doesn't say that the suite holds only 299 cases."},
    {"n": 7, "name": "Evidence fits claims", "score": 1, "reason": "Claim off-lane's own figures (loss shares of 48% and 53%, and September's 65.8% off-lane rate) are not in its block, which draws only per-stratum deltas. Claim cost's caption calls $2.02 September's 'p95', but September's p95 is $5.30 per 1,000 replies. $2.02 is September's mean with its 33 trials that reported no cost filled in at that p95, and the candidates' '$2.24' and '$2.36' are their means with uncosted trials set to zero."}
  ],
  "findings": [
    {"claim": "report", "severity": "must_fix", "finding": "The page ranks the models by cost without naming a provider. The headline ('both candidates cost more per reply'), the recommendation ('the lowest production cost of the three') and two confidence clauses (judge, late aspect) treat the cost gaps (+$0.62 and +$0.49 per 1,000 replies) as properties of the models and as the firm fallback if the judge is wrong. No arm names its provider, and Watch says an earlier round found qwen3.8-flash cheaper. Fix: in the headline and the decision, say these costs are at this run's unnamed routing. Rewrite the judge and late-aspect clauses so they don't present the cost gap as settled, or name the routing caveat and the earlier contrary finding there and say the call holds as the status-quo default until repricing (next step 3)."},
    {"claim": "off-lane", "severity": "should_fix", "finding": "Three fixes here. (1) Carry the reasoning-share confound: the claim reads 1017's holds-character drop (-4.7, -8.2 to -1.3), which that confound touches. (2) Put the claim's own figures in the block: the off-lane loss shares (48% for qwen3.8-flash, 53% for 1017) and September's 65.8%. Right now the block shows only the deltas (-22.9 and -20.2). (3) Change 'where September is weakest' to 'weakest among the larger strata', since 'assistant shape' is lower at 58.8% (6 cases)."},
    {"claim": "cost", "severity": "should_fix", "finding": "The caption says 'September (P) at its own p95 ($2.02 per 1,000 replies)', but September's p95 is $5.30. $2.02 is September's mean cost with its 33 trials that reported no cost filled in at its p95, and $2.24 and $2.36 are the candidates' means with their uncosted trials counted as zero. Describe it that way, as a worst-case fill of the missing costs, so a reader doesn't take $2.02 for a per-reply p95."},
    {"claim": "report", "severity": "should_fix", "finding": "Watch and 'What you could change' measure qwen3.8-flash's headroom on different interval levels. Watch says 2.4 points short of the 15-point line on the corrected interval (-12.6). The table says its verdict turns at a margin of 10.8, which is the 95% reading and gives 4.2 points of room. State both on one basis, or say which level each one uses."},
    {"claim": "report", "severity": "should_fix", "finding": "Next step 4 asks for 815 cases per arm (2,445 trials at three repeats), but the suite has 299 cases. Say that the step needs about 516 new cases, or rephrase it as more repeats, which would not shrink an interval that counts cases."}
  ],
  "resolved": []
}
```"}]}

